# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-error-reported-twice-on-stderr — a failing CAD action prints its message twice
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-25

* Observed

With alfe 2.2.91 (the first version in which a failing =-x= form is
reported at all -- see issues/closed/alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad),
one failing action puts its diagnostic on protocol/stderr.txt twice:

#+begin_example
$ cat protocol/stderr.txt
ERROR protocol request 1: Undefined AutoLISP function NO_SUCH_FUNCTION.
ERROR protocol request 1: Undefined AutoLISP function NO_SUCH_FUNCTION.
#+end_example

* Cause

=autolisp-protocol-server-loop= (source/runtime/autolisp-remote-io.lsp,
FAIL branch) deliberately writes the message to BOTH channels: it calls
=autolisp-log-err= (errors.txt, the session log) and
=autolisp-protocol-write-stderr= (protocol/stderr.txt, the wire). Those
are two different files -- but the bridge block emitted into
run-common.lsp redefines =autolisp-log-err= to write to
=*AUTOLISP_PROTOCOL_STDERRFILE*=, so both land in the same file and the
user sees the line twice.

The duplication has always been in the code; it became visible only
when errors started being reported.

* Fix candidates

1. In the bridge, keep =autolisp-log-err= pointed at the session log
   (errors.txt) and let only =autolisp-protocol-write-stderr= reach the
   wire. Check why the bridge redirected it in the first place -- the
   intent was probably that a CAD-side log line should reach the user
   at all, which the protocol stderr now does.
2. Or, in the loop's FAIL branch, drop the =autolisp-log-err= call when
   the protocol channel is active.

Either way the same message must still appear exactly once, with the
same text, and the =DONE N FAIL= status is unaffected.

* Acceptance

- A failing action writes its diagnostic once.
- A regression test asserts the count, alongside
  protocol-signalling-form-is-reported-fail-not-ok (which already
  hosts the emitted runtime under clautolisp, no CAD needed).

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.104

Fixed in the LOOP, which is the only place that wrote twice. Its two
calls were deliberate and wrote two different files -- the session log
(=autolisp-log-err=) and the wire (=autolisp-protocol-write-stderr=) --
but run-common's bridge REDIRECTS =autolisp-log-err= onto the protocol
stderr so CAD-side warnings reach the user at all, and the loop always
runs with that bridge. Both calls therefore landed in the same file.

The loop now writes the wire only, in both places that reported a
failure (the read error and the request error). The bridge keeps
carrying everything else =autolisp-log-err= is used for, so no warning
is lost; nothing else changes, because with the bridge in place the two
calls were writing the same bytes to the same file.

Test: =protocol-a-failure-is-reported-exactly-once= (hosted under
clautolisp) counts the =ERROR protocol request= lines for one failing
request and requires exactly one, with the engine's message still in it.
alfe suite 1200 checks, 0 failures.
